posts.body:
A deadly virus is on the streets, pouncing and preying for fresh lives. Due to this novel gangsta, you are compelled to stay indoors (for the people who have homes, of course). But as long as you have your phone and wifi running, you don't really care about you-know-what.

Let's consider another situation. You wake up, check your phone and, to your astonishment and utter disbelief, find that there is not a single notification. You curse your data provider, go to your balcony for the towers and find that military personnel have been heavily deployed. Bewildered, as anyone would be, you just wish that the internet comes back soon. To not let my protagonist (that is you) suffer too much, I bring back the internet within a week. But what if it doesn't come back for 100 days?

The petition filed by Anuradha Bhasin against the sudden imposition of curfew in the newly created UT of Jammu and Kashmir is a far better explanation of the condition of the internet shutdown in the Valley. The haunting diary entries annexed to the petition would not only make a third party empathetic towards their plight but also make the petitioner's (a newspaper agency) case stronger. Kashmir Times, the petitioner's newspaper, through its petition tried to establish the following;

a) that the aforementioned newspaper agency was not able to publish its paper in the Srinagar region, which implies a redundant and unreasonable restriction of freedom of speech and expression;

b) that at some point during the day mobile phone networks, internet services, and landline phone connectivity were cut off, thus isolating the region without any formal orders;

c) that this sudden and grave action of the authorities led to a virtual blackout which creates uncertainty in the minds of the people.

The reason for our heightened empathy is that we can very well picture ourselves in a lockdown without internet. So, in a way, Kashmiris were living in a curfew (not a lockdown) before the pandemic hit us. Every chord of the petitioner's plea strikes with us unless, of course, you hear the other side.

This case can be used to explain how anecdotal evidence is never enough in a courtroom. I am saying this because the government's reply to the petition is in total contradiction to the style adopted by the petitioners. That is, the former emphasized facts and figures, many a time showing the other side of the coin, and put on record evidence showcasing the urgent need for shutting down the internet. Whereas the latter chose the right words to make an emotional plea.

I will be dealing with the government's side of the story in my next post, but before I sign off I want you to go to the top of the page, read the title again, and try to answer it before the government changes your mind!
